														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2338626">
									

<p>My issues with this one:</p>
<ul>
<li>I dislike compulsion effects as a general rule. I feel they are a way around doing the heavy lifting of giving characters and events proper context and motivation, and are sort of a cheap way to a tragic/sad ending. I can expound in much, much greater detail on this point if anyone wishes.</li>
<li>I'm so very tired of the metacommentary on how heartless the Foundation is. The business with the thing not liking being addressed by a number felt forced and unnecessary. Also, given that you have a Foundation agent just straight walking into a harmful anomaly and going &quot;hey sup&quot;, they're going to draw some line of formality about names? Really?</li>
<li>For that matter, why are staff put into this thing? The containment procedures state that only Foundation personnel can enter, but there's no justification for that. Which makes it read to me as purely a contrivance for that last interview.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is very clearly an overt attempt to deliver a cat-based feels missile, which makes it feel manipulative and also means that the narrative is constructed with only that purpose in mind, leaving aside matters like how much actual sense it makes.</p>

